From 2ce2bfb1cdb1e8a614dc43109fcbca000b9de983 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:16:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 36/38] feat(scheduler): survive a prefill CUDA OOM instead of
 killing the lane
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Today one allocation failure destroys every healthy in-flight request. The
traceback of the 2026-09-30 16:44:17 death shows why: torch.OutOfMemoryError is
raised in qwen_sparse_attn_backend.forward_extend, and there is NO try/except
anywhere between run_batch (scheduler.py:1988) and the top of the process. It
lands in run_scheduler_process's bare `except Exception` (scheduler.py:6090),
which does parent_process.send_signal(SIGQUIT); the parent's
running_phase_sigquit_handler then calls kill_process_tree(os.getpid()). The KV
pool was only 73% full at the time -- the shortage was driver-level VRAM outside
the pool -- so nothing about the request mix justified losing the lane.

WHAT THIS DOES. Catches torch.OutOfMemoryError at the batch boundary, releases
the offending prefill batch, requeues its requests, and continues the event loop.

WHY IT IS BELIEVED SAFE. Every resource is already committed before the forward
starts (prepare_for_extend runs at scheduler.py:4108, run_batch at :1988), and
the teardown for exactly this set already exists and is already exercised by
pause_generation(mode="retract"): retract_all -> release_req ->
release_kv_cache + reset_for_retract covers the KV pages, the req_to_token slot,
the mamba/GDN state slot, the radix lock ref and inflight_middle_chunks.
retract_all was already imported at scheduler.py:217. The radix cache cannot hold
a partial node because maybe_cache_unfinished_req only runs post-forward, and
prefill takes no CUDA graph here (--cuda-graph-backend-prefill=disabled).

THE LOAD-BEARING HAZARD IS STREAM ORDERING, NOT BOOKKEEPING. The exception skips
_apply_war_barrier(), so earlier layers' kernels are still enqueued on
forward_stream and still READ req_to_token and the KV pages this frees, while the
scheduler writes on schedule_stream. The recovery therefore drains
forward_stream FIRST and re-establishes the WAR fence before freeing anything.
Get that order wrong and a freed page is handed to the next allocation while a
live kernel still reads it: a healthy peer's output silently corrupts. That is
strictly worse than the crash this replaces, which is why the drain failing at
all returns False and lets the lane die as before.

SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. Only torch.OutOfMemoryError -- raised host-side by
the caching allocator before the failing kernel launches, so the CUDA context is
intact -- and only on an extend batch. The 2026-09-29 death was a different
class: ncclUnhandledCudaError "Failed to CUDA calloc 536870912 bytes" from
sampler.py:687, which leaves the communicator undefined. Catching that would keep
a wedged process serving garbage. It stays fatal, by construction.

SGLANG_OOM_MAX_REQ_RETRACTIONS (default 3) aborts a request with a 503 after
repeated retractions. Without it a request whose context can never fit re-admits
and re-OOMs forever: no forward progress, no crash and no alert -- a worse outage
than the one being fixed.

⛔ WHY THIS CANNOT SHIP ON INSPECTION. SGLANG_ENABLE_STRICT_MEM_CHECK_DURING_IDLE
defaults to True, and invariant_checker.py:479 raises on a pool leak. So if
retract_all misses any resource, the lane still dies -- later, at the next idle
moment, through the same SIGQUIT path, with a traceback pointing at the checker
instead of at the OOM. A leaky version of this patch is harder to diagnose than
no patch at all.

REQUIRED BEFORE IT SHIPS:
  1. Forced-OOM injection in the prefill forward: the loop continues and the
     request completes on retry.
  2. THE DECISIVE ONE: >= 20 forced prefill OOMs, then drive to idle with
     SGLANG_ENABLE_STRICT_MEM_CHECK_DURING_IDLE=1 and assert _check_all_pools,
     _check_req_pool and verify_byte_accounting all pass. This is what proves no
     leak in any of the four pools.
  3. OOM while chunked_req is set: inflight_middle_chunks == 0 and
     chunked_req is None afterwards.
  4. mamba_allocator.available_size() returns to baseline (this lane runs
     --max-mamba-cache-size 36).
  5. The matched prefix node's lock_ref returns to its pre-admission value and
     the prefix stays evictable.
  6. The NEXTN / eagle_worker_v2 path specifically -- the OOM occurred under
     _forward_prefill_batch, so the DRAFT worker's state needs re-verifying, not
     just the target's. FutureMap ring slots are the known residual risk: if the
     OOM lands before publish(), those req_pool_indices are unpublished. The
     existing pause_generation retract path does not reset future_map either, so
     the assumption is shared with shipped code -- but it is unproven.
  7. Livelock guard: a request that OOMs unconditionally is aborted with 503
     after SGLANG_OOM_MAX_REQ_RETRACTIONS, not requeued forever.
  8. The existing retract/pause suites unchanged (they share retract_all).
  9. Peers in running_batch produce bit-identical output across a forced OOM.

NOTE THIS IS SURVIVABILITY, NOT PREVENTION. The structural cause is upstream of
the scheduler: qwen_sparse_attn_backend.py:1450-1477 gathers the FULL sequence
length per chunk (index_select over req_to_token[.., :sequence_lens[i]], then
torch.cat), so four full-context dense transients are live per full-attention
layer per forward and peak non-pool VRAM scales with total context, not with
--chunked-prefill-size. Bounding that gather is the actual fix, and this tree
already contains the template upstream added for the same bug class in the DSA
backend (63d320c723, mqa_logits_utils.py + SGLANG_DSA_MQA_LOGITS_FREE_MEM_FRACTION).
---
 python/sglang/srt/environ.py            |   5 +
 python/sglang/srt/managers/scheduler.py | 123 +++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 127 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/python/sglang/srt/environ.py b/python/sglang/srt/environ.py
index af7d5c3..15a5a43 100644
--- a/python/sglang/srt/environ.py
+++ b/python/sglang/srt/environ.py
@@ -627,6 +627,11 @@ class Envs:
     SGLANG_SCHEDULER_RECV_SKIPPER_WEIGHT_NONE = EnvInt(1)
     # in seconds. Set if you observe high memory accumulation over a long serving period.
     SGLANG_EMPTY_CACHE_INTERVAL = EnvFloat(-1)
+    # Abort a request after this many prefill-OOM retractions rather than
+    # requeueing it forever. Without a cap, a request whose context can never
+    # fit re-admits and re-OOMs indefinitely: no forward progress, no crash and
+    # no alert, which is a worse outage than the crash it replaced.
+    SGLANG_OOM_MAX_REQ_RETRACTIONS = EnvInt(3)
     SGLANG_SCHEDULER_MAX_RECV_PER_POLL = EnvInt(-1)
     SGLANG_SCHEDULER_SKIP_ALL_GATHER = EnvBool(False)
     SGLANG_SCHEDULER_DECREASE_PREFILL_IDLE = EnvBool(False)
diff --git a/python/sglang/srt/managers/scheduler.py b/python/sglang/srt/managers/scheduler.py
index a7d8bae..2797cb5 100644
--- a/python/sglang/srt/managers/scheduler.py
+++ b/python/sglang/srt/managers/scheduler.py
@@ -1985,7 +1985,29 @@ class Scheduler(
 
             # Launch the current batch
             if batch:
-                batch_result = self.run_batch(batch)
+                # ⛔ A PREFILL OOM MUST NOT KILL THE LANE.
+                # Without this, torch.OutOfMemoryError escapes every frame up to
+                # run_scheduler_process's bare `except Exception`, which sends
+                # SIGQUIT to the parent, whose handler calls
+                # kill_process_tree(os.getpid()) -- so one 560 MiB allocation
+                # failure destroys every healthy in-flight request. Measured
+                # 2026-09-30 16:44:17 with the KV pool only 73% full.
+                #
+                # Scope is deliberately minimal: ONLY a host-side
+                # torch.OutOfMemoryError (raised by the caching allocator before
+                # the failing kernel launches, so the CUDA context is intact),
+                # and ONLY on an extend batch. A driver-level failure -- the
+                # 2026-09-29 `ncclUnhandledCudaError: Failed to CUDA calloc` --
+                # leaves the communicator undefined and MUST still be fatal;
+                # surviving it would serve garbage.
+                try:
+                    batch_result = self.run_batch(batch)
+                except torch.OutOfMemoryError:
+                    if not batch.forward_mode.is_extend():
+                        raise
+                    if not self._drop_prefill_batch_on_oom(batch):
+                        raise
+                    continue
                 # Fence result processing behind this forward's shared reads.
                 self._apply_war_barrier()
                 self.result_queue.append((batch.copy(), batch_result))
@@ -4290,6 +4312,105 @@ class Scheduler(
         # GenerationBatchResult.extra_keep_alive_refs after forward returns.
         self.batch_record_buf[self.batch_record_ct] = [batch, attr_snapshot]
 
+    def _drop_prefill_batch_on_oom(self, batch: ScheduleBatch) -> bool:
+        """Release a prefill batch whose forward OOM'd, and requeue its requests.
+
+        Returns False to decline recovery, in which case the caller re-raises and
+        the lane dies as before. Declining is always the safe answer.
+
+        ⛔ THE ORDER OF THE FIRST TWO STEPS IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT. The
+        exception skipped _apply_war_barrier(), so earlier layers' kernels are
+        still enqueued on forward_stream and still READ req_to_token and the KV
+        pages this method is about to free, while the scheduler's own writes run
+        on schedule_stream. Freeing before draining hands live pages to the next
+        allocation and corrupts a healthy peer's output instead of dropping one
+        request -- a silent wrong answer in place of a loud crash, which is worse
+        than the bug being fixed.
+        """
+        # 1. Drain the aborted forward before touching anything it may read.
+        try:
+            self.forward_stream.synchronize()
+        except Exception:
+            logger.exception("Prefill OOM: could not drain forward_stream; "
+                             "declining recovery so the lane fails loudly.")
+            return False
+        runner = getattr(self.model_worker, "last_shared_read_runner", None)
+        if runner is not None:
+            runner.shared_read_done_event = None
+        self.schedule_stream.wait_stream(self.forward_stream)
+
+        # 2. run_batch never appended this batch's result, so result_queue and
+        #    last_batch would drift apart: pop_and_process() pops one entry
+        #    whenever last_batch is set. Drain the previous entry and clear.
+        if self.last_batch is not None and self.result_queue:
+            tmp_batch, tmp_result = self.result_queue.popleft()
+            self.process_batch_result(tmp_batch, tmp_result)
+        self.last_batch = None
+        self.cur_batch_for_debug = None
+
+        # 3. A request whose context can never fit would otherwise re-admit and
+        #    re-OOM forever: no progress, no crash, no alert. Cap it.
+        max_retractions = envs.SGLANG_OOM_MAX_REQ_RETRACTIONS.get()
+        reqs, doomed = [], []
+        for req in batch.reqs:
+            if req.finished():
+                continue
+            reqs.append(req)
+            if req.retraction_count >= max_retractions:
+                doomed.append(req)
+
+        # 4. Release KV pages, the req_to_token slot, the mamba/GDN state slot,
+        #    the radix lock ref and inflight_middle_chunks. retract_all ->
+        #    release_req -> release_kv_cache + reset_for_retract covers all five;
+        #    this is the same teardown pause_generation(mode="retract") uses.
+        if reqs:
+            retract_all(
+                reqs=reqs,
+                req_to_token_pool=self.req_to_token_pool,
+                token_to_kv_pool_allocator=self.token_to_kv_pool_allocator,
+                tree_cache=self.tree_cache,
+                hisparse_coordinator=self.hisparse_coordinator,
+                offload_kv=False,
+            )
+        if self.chunked_req is not None and self.chunked_req in batch.reqs:
+            self.chunked_req = None
+        self.running_batch.batch_is_full = False
+
+        # 5. Hand cached-but-unallocated segments back to the driver, so the
+        #    retry -- and any lazy cubin load -- has headroom outside the pool.
+        #    With expandable_segments this may return very little.
+        try:
+            current_platform.empty_cache()
+        except Exception:
+            logger.debug("Prefill OOM: empty_cache failed", exc_info=True)
+
+        for req in doomed:
+            self.ipc_channels.send_to_tokenizer.send_output(
+                _make_abort_req(
+                    req,
+                    finished_reason={
+                        "type": "abort",
+                        "status_code": HTTPStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
+                        "message": (
+                            "Prefill ran out of device memory "
+                            f"{req.retraction_count} times; giving up on this "
+                            "request rather than wedging the lane."
+                        ),
+                    },
+                ),
+                req,
+            )
+        for req in reqs:
+            if req not in doomed:
+                self._add_request_to_queue(req, is_retracted=True)
+
+        logger.error(
+            "Prefill OOM recovered: batch of %d req(s) dropped, %d aborted, "
+            "%d requeued. The lane stayed up.",
+            len(batch.reqs), len(doomed), len(reqs) - len(doomed),
+        )
+        return True
+
     @contextmanager
     def _forward_isolation(self, batch: ScheduleBatch, *, overlap: bool):
         """Make SB transactional across one forward (overlap and non-overlap).
